  "note": "Re-judged 2026-10-27 at HEAD 58ee58c176 (independent judge, clean git clone --shared, go -overlay mapping ike_auth_algorithms_test.go away for journal row 3f1a944493, tree untouched), after 0308ef4d70 and 690d38f274. RFC 3948 Section 5.1: \"Because SGW will now see two possible SAs that lead to 10.1.2.3, it can become confused about where to send packets coming from Suzy's server.  Implementors MUST devise ways of preventing this from occurring.\" Ze's way is the cross-peer inner-selector registry (narrowChildSelectors filter, innerSelectorRegistry.claim at install): a tunnel-mode pair one packet could match together with another peer's live pair is refused TS_UNACCEPTABLE unless both pairs are operator-fixed and their policy-priority values differ. 0308ef4d70 only dropped childPolicyPair's unused remote-half return; childPolicies and innerSelectorPairOf still build from the one childPolicyPair, so the registry compares the policy the dataplane installs, and with innerSelectorPairOf forced to report no pair 37 tagged and untagged units go red. 690d38f274 closes the three unproven guards the 5064d6db1f verdict named, each reproduced here by overlay break: classifyInnerPair's `if p.transport()` forced true reddens TestRFC3948TunnelPeerOwnHostPairBesideFullTunnelRefused alone; samePolicyPair's local-port test forced false reddens TestRFC3948ConfiguredSelectorNarrowedOnLocalPortRefused alone; its local-prefix test forced false reddens TestRFC3948ConfiguredSelectorNarrowedOnLocalPrefixRefused alone. Each runs both responder steps (narrowChildSelectors then createFirstChildSA), and mustSecondRefused asserts the first served, the second refused as TS_UNACCEPTABLE with the RFC 3948 Section 5.1 reason, nothing on its dataplane and the registry holding one Child SA, so the refusal cannot come from another rule. Isolation holds: each second peer's pair differs from its operator-fixed form by exactly the one dimension the break ignores, and under the break the pair is served at the distinct priorities, which is the red. These three enforce Ze's stated rule (a pair is operator-fixed only when it is the whole configured selector, and the configured-host pair orders a transport-mode pair only), which is stricter than the bare MUST needs, since the SPD would order the overlaps they refuse; they are legitimate Section 5.1 negatives because they pin the edges of the mechanism Ze devised and the rfc/short Support remaining row claims, and they assert nothing the RFC contradicts. RE-PROBED at HEAD: conflictLocked overlap test forced to skip reddens 37 units; operatorOrdered forced true reddens TestRFC3948ConfiguredOverlapEqualPriorityRefused and TestRFC3948OrderedTransportEqualPriorityRefused. The earlier verdict's probes of claim atomicity, initiator wiring, IKE and Child SA rekey carriers, stranded records and the lease guards cover units this window did not change and are not repeated. Not counted: replacing childPolicyPair's intersectProto by either half's protocol leaves every RFC3948-5.1-1 unit green, because responder narrowing intersects each half against the policy; the installed intersection is proven by RFC4301-4.4.2-2's mutant record on TestRFC4301MixedProtocolPairInstallsTheIntersection, and the registry reads the same function. Positives: TestRFC3948TwoUnboundPeersDistinctInnerAddressesServed, TestRFC3948OrderedTransportBesideFullTunnelServed, the two lease tests in cp_test.go and the Ze-initiated rekey lease test, each a conforming input, not a different error.",
